{"name":"Eiger Nordwand","hex":"#7799bb","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[],"entries":[{"title":"North face of the Eiger","blocks":[{"text":"The Eiger is part of the Bernese Alps in Switzerland, and its north face rises over 1,800 meters (5,900 ft). With alpine climbing routes that are up to four kilometers long, the face hosts some of the Alps' longest and most demanding climbing routes, with significant risks from rockfall and avalanches. The wall gained fame through dramatic climbing attempts and ascents, heightened by its visibility from Grindelwald and, even more clearly, from the Wengernalp Railway-accessible Kleine Scheidegg."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2c/Thunersee%2C_Eiger%2C_M%C3%B6nch_und_Jungfrau.jpg/330px-Thunersee%2C_Eiger%2C_M%C3%B6nch_und_Jungfrau.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/North_face_of_the_Eiger"},{"title":"Eiger","blocks":[{"text":"The Eiger is a 3,967-metre (13,015 ft) mountain of the Bernese Alps, overlooking Grindelwald and Lauterbrunnen in the Bernese Oberland of Switzerland, just north of the main watershed and border with Valais. It is the easternmost peak of a ridge crest that extends across the Mönch to the Jungfrau at 4,158 m (13,642 ft), constituting one of the most emblematic sights of the Swiss Alps. While the northern side of the mountain rises more than 3,000 m (10,000 ft) above the two valleys of Grindelwald and Lauterbrunnen, the southern side faces the large glaciers of the Jungfrau-Aletsch area, the most glaciated region in the Alps. The most notable feature of the Eiger is its nearly 1,800-metre-high (5,900 ft) north face of rock and ice, the biggest north face in the Alps. This substantial face towers over the resort of Kleine Scheidegg at its base, on the eponymous pass connecting the two valleys."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fc/North_face.jpg/330px-North_face.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eiger"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c7/Eiger_Pilz-Mushroom.jpg/500px-Eiger_Pilz-Mushroom.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c7/Eiger_Pilz-Mushroom.jpg/500px-Eiger_Pilz-Mushroom.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Stratification in the North Face. From this \"Mushroom,\" Ueli Gegenschatz performed a BASE jump in 2000.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AEiger_Pilz-Mushroom.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/10/Eiger_2415.jpg/500px-Eiger_2415.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/10/Eiger_2415.jpg/1280px-Eiger_2415.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/10/Eiger_2415.jpg/500px-Eiger_2415.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The Eiger-Nordwand viewed from the northwest.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AEiger_2415.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/75/Eiger_NE_NW_FromEastOfGrindelwald.jpg/500px-Eiger_NE_NW_FromEastOfGrindelwald.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/75/Eiger_NE_NW_FromEastOfGrindelwald.jpg/500px-Eiger_NE_NW_FromEastOfGrindelwald.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Northeast and Eiger-Nordwand, separated by the North Pillar","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AEiger_NE_NW_FromEastOfGrindelwald.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/93/Gr